      "problem": "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨5{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩,$ then $F(t)=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨\\text{ }5{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨5{t}^{4},\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int 5{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}})\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{5}+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{1}{t}+{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{5},\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration.",
      "answer_fields": [
