    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-349150",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3081ffe7-c448-5e0d-8dd1-808d23199a7f",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Definite Integral of a Vector-Valued Function",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral of a vector-valued function. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-1}^{3}⟨{t}^{2}+t,\\,2{t}^{3}-t⟩\\,\\text{d}t=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}⟨{t}^{2}+t,\\,2{t}^{3}-t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{-1}^{3}({t}^{2}+t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(2{t}^{3}-t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first component,\n\n$$\n\\int ({t}^{2}+t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFor the second component,\n\n$$\n\\int (2{t}^{3}-t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}⟨{t}^{2}+t,\\,2{t}^{3}-t⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨(\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}){|}_{-1}^{3},\\,(\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}){|}_{-1}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(\\frac{1}{3}(27)+\\frac{1}{2}(9))-(\\frac{1}{3}(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}(1)),\\,(\\frac{1}{2}(81)-\\frac{1}{2}(9))-(\\frac{1}{2}(1)-\\frac{1}{2}(1))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(9+\\frac{9}{2})-(-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(36)-0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{40}{3},\\,36⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{40}{3},\\,36⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
