      "math_academy_id": "q-349125",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3081ffe7-c448-5e0d-8dd1-808d23199a7f",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Definite Integral of a Vector-Valued Function",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral of a vector-valued function. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{4}⟨2{t}^{2}-t,\\,3{e}^{-t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t=⟨$\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}⟨2{t}^{2}-t,\\,3{e}^{-t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨{\\int }_{0}^{4}(2{t}^{2}-t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,{\\int }_{0}^{4}3{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the first component,\n\n$$\n\\int (2{t}^{2}-t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFor the second component,\n\n$$\n\\int 3{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=3\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})=-3{e}^{-t}.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}⟨2{t}^{2}-t,\\,3{e}^{-t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{4},\\,(-3{e}^{-t}){|}_{0}^{4}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(\\frac{2}{3}(64)-\\frac{1}{2}(16))-0,\\,(-3{e}^{-4})-(-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(\\frac{128}{3}-8),\\,3(1-{e}^{-4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{104}{3},\\,3(1-{e}^{-4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{104}{3},\\,3(1-{e}^{-4})⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
