    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-99586",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8dd18a5b-d7a1-5590-ae70-f47ff5b464b1",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function That Satisfies a Given Initial Condition",
      "problem": "If ${F}^{′}(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t}+t,\\,{2}^{t}⟩$ and $F(1)=⟨2,\\,0⟩,$ then $F(t)=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨\\sqrt{t}+t,\\,{2}^{t}⟩\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\sqrt{t}+t,\\,{2}^{t}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int (\\sqrt{t}+t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int {2}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{{2}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe initial condition $F(1)=⟨2,\\,0⟩$ gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1)=⟨\\frac{2(1)^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{{2}^{1}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\frac{7}{6}+{c}_{1},\\,\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{5}{6}$ and ${c}_{2}=-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$ Consequently\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =⟨\\frac{2{t}^{3/2}}{3}+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{6},\\,\\frac{{2}^{t}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4{t}^{3/2}+3{t}^{2}+5}{6},\\,\\frac{{2}^{t}-2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
