    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-349355",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8dd18a5b-d7a1-5590-ae70-f47ff5b464b1",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function That Satisfies a Given Initial Condition",
      "problem": "Find $F(t)$ given that \n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=⟨\\frac{4}{t+1}\\,,3\\sqrt{t}⟩\n$$\n\n and $F(0)=⟨2,\\,-1⟩.$\n\n$F(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨\\frac{4}{t+1}\\,,3\\sqrt{t}⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\frac{4}{t+1}\\,,3\\sqrt{t}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int \\frac{4}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 3\\sqrt{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+{c}_{1},\\,2{t}^{\\frac{3}{2}}+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F(0)=⟨2,\\,-1⟩$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0)=⟨4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+{c}_{1},\\,2(0)^{\\frac{3}{2}}+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨{c}_{1},\\,{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=-1.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+2,\\,2{t}^{3/2}-1⟩.\n$$",
