    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-349193",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8dd18a5b-d7a1-5590-ae70-f47ff5b464b1",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function That Satisfies a Given Initial Condition",
      "problem": "Find $F(t)$ given that \n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\frac{4}{{t}^{3/2}}\\,,\\frac{4}{t+1}⟩\n$$\n\n and $F(1)=⟨2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)⟩.$\n\n$F(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨-\\frac{4}{{t}^{3/2}}\\,,\\frac{4}{t+1}⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨-\\frac{4}{{t}^{3/2}}\\,,\\frac{4}{t+1}⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int -\\frac{4}{{t}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\frac{4}{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{t}}+{c}_{1},\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F(1)=⟨2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)⟩$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(1)=⟨\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{1}}+{c}_{1},\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8+{c}_{1},\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨2,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=-6$ and ${c}_{2}=0.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{t}}-6,\\,4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)⟩.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
