    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-347095",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8dd18a5b-d7a1-5590-ae70-f47ff5b464b1",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function That Satisfies a Given Initial Condition",
      "problem": "Find $F(t)$ given that \n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=⟨-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩\n$$\n\n and $F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨4,\\,-2⟩.$\n\n$F(t)=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$⟩$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that \n\n$$\nF(t)=\\int {F}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\int ⟨-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨-\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)⟩\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int -\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨4,\\,-2⟩$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=⟨\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{c}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨4,\\,-2⟩ \\\\ ⟨1+{c}_{1},\\,-1+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨4,\\,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${c}_{1}=3$ and ${c}_{2}=-1.$ Consequently\n\n$$\nF(t)=⟨\\operatorname{csc}⁡(t)+3,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1⟩.\n$$",
