    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-615",
      "knowledge_point_id": "8dd18a5b-d7a1-5590-ae70-f47ff5b464b1",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function That Satisfies a Given Initial Condition",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\mathbf F(t)$ given that $\\mathbf F'(t)= \\left\\langle \\sec t \\tan t, \\,  \\sin(2t)\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf F(0)=\\langle 0,\\, 0\\rangle.$",
      "worked_solution": "Note that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle \\sec t \\tan t,\\,  \\sin(2t)\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+{c}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition $\\mathbf F(0)=\\left\\langle 0,\\, 0 \\right\\rangle$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0)=⟨\\operatorname{sec}⁡0+{c}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨0,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨1+{c}_{1},\\,-\\frac{1}{2}+{c}_{2}⟩ & =⟨0,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $c_1=-1$ and $c_2=\\dfrac 12.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\left\\langle  \\sec t - 1  , \\,  -\\dfrac 1 2\\cos(2t)+ \\dfrac12\\right\\rangle.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
  ]
